  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "How the linear velocity $v$ of an electron in the Bohr orbit is related to its quantum number $m$",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "s6ZHp",
      "text": "$v \\propto \\frac{1}{n}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "y607a",
      "text": "$v \\propto \\frac{1}{n^{2}}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "c2xKI",
      "text": "$v \\propto \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "nSqGE",
      "text": "$v \\times n$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\because$ Linear velocity of an electron in a orbit of H like atom,<br />$v=218 \\times 10^{6} \\times \\frac{Z}{n} \\mathrm{m} / \\mathrm{s}=\\frac{c_{0}}{137} \\cdot \\frac{Z}{n}$<br />where, $Z=$ number of protons in nucleus, $n=$ principal quantum number and $c_{o}=$ speed of light in free space, Thus, we have<br />$v \\propto \\frac{1}{n}$",
    "image": null
  },
